// Blonde Ambition Blue Grama — Bouteloua gracilis 'Blonde Ambition'
// Edit this file to update plant-specific content.

#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Blonde Ambition Blue Grama", "Bouteloua gracilis 'Blonde Ambition'", "grama")[
  'Blonde Ambition' is a Colorado native warm-season grass selected for its exceptional ornamental eyelash-shaped seed heads that mature to golden-blonde in late summer. Extraordinary drought tolerance, good fall color, and wildlife value. Grows 18–24 inches tall with seed heads reaching 24–30 inches. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation. NOTE: Clay drainage is a consideration.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with a *0.5 gph emitter* — the minimum during establishment. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule. Blonde Ambition Blue Grama is a native prairie grass and extremely drought tolerant. The 0.5 gph emitter on the shared schedule delivers a conservative amount well-suited to this plant. Once fully established, drip support may not be necessary at all.

=== Fertilization

No fertilization needed. Blue Grama performs best with minimal to no feeding — this aligns with your high-P soil. If anything, a very light application of potassium sulfate (0-0-50) in late spring is acceptable. Never use balanced or high-nitrogen formulas. Lean soil mimics its native habitat best.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Cut back to 3–4 inches in late winter to early spring (late February–March) before new growth emerges. Do not cut in fall — seed heads provide critical winter interest and wildlife food value. As a warm-season grass, it begins growth late in spring — do not be alarmed by a slow start in April.

=== Wildlife Value

Blue Grama seed heads are a significant food source for house finches and American robins in late summer and fall. The seed-eating habit of these birds is well matched to this grass's abundant seed production. Northern flickers may forage at the base of the clumps for insects. The clump structure provides modest ground-level shelter for cottontail rabbits. Hummingbirds and butterflies are not typically attracted to grass flowers, though the beneficial insect community supported by native grasses benefits pollinators indirectly.

=== Pests & Disease

Blonde Ambition Blue Grama has essentially no serious pest or disease problems in Colorado. Occasional #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=blue+grama+rust+fungus+treatment")[rust fungus] may appear as orange streaks on leaves in wet conditions — usually resolves without treatment in Colorado's dry climate. In clay soil, ensure good drainage at the planting zone as noted in Other Care. This is one of the most problem-free plants in your landscape.

=== Other Care

Clay soil note: Plant in a raised or sloped area and amend the planting zone with coarse grit to ensure drainage. Divide clumps every 4–5 years if they die out in the center. No serious pest or disease problems. The unique seed heads are most ornamental from August through winter — resist the urge to cut them off early.
